(There are certain terms used in connection with stock speculation that) Tj T*
(are very familiar to those who come in contact with stock brokers, and) Tj T*
(yet are not always familiar to those who do business by mail.) Tj T*
(Undoubtedly the majority of our readers are familiar with these terms,) Tj T*
(but we give these definitions for the benefit of the few who are not) Tj T*
(familiar with them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Trader: A person who buys and sells stocks is usually referred to as a) Tj T*
(trader. The word probably originated when it was customary to trade one) Tj T*
(stock for another and later was used to refer to a person who sold one) Tj T*
(stock and bought another. He was a trader; but the person who buys) Tj T*
(stocks for a profit and sells them and takes his profit when he gets an) Tj T*
(opportunity, may not be a trader in the strict sense of the word.) Tj T*
(However, for convenience, we use the word "trader" in this book to refer) Tj T*
(to any one who buys or sells stocks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Speculator: This word refers to a person who buys stocks for profit,) Tj T*
(with the expectation of selling at a higher price, without reference to) Tj T*
(the earnings of the stock. He may sell first, with the expectation of) Tj T*
(buying at a lower price, as explained in Chapter XVII. on "Short) Tj T*
(Selling." In many cases where we use the word "trader," it would be more) Tj T*
(correct to use the word "speculator.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Investor: An investor differs from a speculator in the fact that he buys) Tj T*
(stocks or bonds with the expectation of holding them for some time for) Tj T*
(the income to be derived from them, without reference to their) Tj T*
(speculative possibilities. We believe that investors always should give) Tj T*
(some consideration to the speculative possibilities of their purchases.) Tj T*
(It frequently is possible to get speculative profits without increase of) Tj T*
(risk or loss of income.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Bull: One who believes that the market price of stocks will advance is) Tj T*
(called a bull. Of course, it is possible to be a bull in one stock and a) Tj T*
(bear in another. The word is used very frequently with reference to the) Tj T*
(market, a bull market meaning a rising market.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Bear: The opposite of a bull is a bear. It refers to a person who) Tj T*
(believes that the market value of stocks will decline, and a bear market) Tj T*
(is a declining market.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lambs: "Lambs" refers to that part of the public that knows so little) Tj T*
(about stock speculating that they lose all their money sooner or later.) Tj T*
(The bulls and bears get them going and coming. If the lambs would read) Tj T*
(this book carefully, they would discover reasons why they lose their) Tj T*
(money.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Long and Short: Those who +own+ stocks are said to be long, and those) Tj T*
(who +owe+ stocks are said to be short. Short selling is explained in) Tj T*
(Chapter XVII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Odd Lot: Stocks on exchanges are sold in certain lots. On the New York) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange, 100 shares is a lot; and on the Consolidated Stock) Tj T*
ET
endstream
