(Exchange, 10 shares is a lot. Less than these amounts is an odd lot.) Tj T*
(When you sell an odd lot you usually get 1/8 less than the market price;) Tj T*
(and when you buy an odd lot, you usually pay 1/8 more than the market) Tj T*
(price; that is, 1/8 of a dollar on each share where prices are quoted in) Tj T*
(dollars.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Point: It is a common expression to say that a stock went up or down a) Tj T*
(point, which means a dollar in a stock that is quoted in dollars, but a) Tj T*
(cent in a stock that is quoted in cents, as many of the stocks are on) Tj T*
(the New York Curb. In cotton quotations, a point is 1/100 part of a) Tj T*
(cent. For instance, if cotton is quoted at 18.12, it means 18 cents and) Tj T*
(12/100 of a cent per pound, and if it went up 30 points the quotation) Tj T*
(would be 18.42.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Reaction: Every person who has traded in listed stocks probably is) Tj T*
(familiar with this word. It means to act in an opposite direction, but) Tj T*
(it is used especially to refer to a decline in the price of a stock that) Tj T*
(has been going up.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Rally: "Rally" is the opposite of the sense in which "reaction" usually) Tj T*
(is used. When a stock is going down and it turns and goes up, it is) Tj T*
(called a rally.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Commitment: This term is used referring to a purchase of stock. It is) Tj T*
(more commonly used by investment bankers when they contract to buy an) Tj T*
(issue, but the term sometimes is used by traders.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Floating Supply: The stock of a company that is in the hands of that) Tj T*
(part of the public who is likely to sell, is referred to as floating) Tj T*
(supply.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER IV.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A CORRECT BASIS FOR SPECULATING) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We maintain that there is only one basis upon which successful) Tj T*
(speculation can be carried on continually; that is, never to buy a) Tj T*
(security unless it is selling at a price below that which is warranted) Tj T*
(by assets, earning power, and prospective future earning power.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are many influences that affect the movements of stock prices,) Tj T*
(which are referred to in subsequent chapters. All of these should be) Tj T*
(studied and understood, but they should be used as secondary factors in) Tj T*
(relation to the value of the stock in which you are trading.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the market price of any stock is far below its intrinsic value and) Tj T*
(there is no reason why the future should bring about a change in this) Tj T*
(value that will decrease it, then you may be certain that important) Tj T*
ET
