(you select. We would first of all divide all stocks into two classes,) Tj T*
(those listed on the New York Stock Exchange and those not listed on the) Tj T*
(New York Stock Exchange. As a rule, it is better to buy stocks listed on) Tj T*
(the New York Stock Exchange, although there are frequent exceptions to) Tj T*
(this rule.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then, the stocks listed on the New York Stock Exchange may be divided) Tj T*
(into classes, such as railroad stocks, public utility stocks, motor) Tj T*
(stocks, tire stocks, oil stocks, copper stocks, gold stocks, and so) Tj T*
(forth. At certain times certain stocks are in a much more favorable) Tj T*
(condition than at other times. In 1919, when the industrial stocks were) Tj T*
(selling at a very high price, the public utility stocks and gold stocks) Tj T*
(were selling low, because it was impossible to increase incomes in) Tj T*
(proportion to the increase in operating costs. But since the beginning) Tj T*
(of 1921, the condition of these two classes of stocks has been improving) Tj T*
(and the market has reflected that improvement.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At the time of this writing \(early in April, 1922\) we are recommending) Tj T*
(the stocks of only a very few manufacturing companies; but we are) Tj T*
(recommending a number \(not all\) of the railroad and public utility) Tj T*
(stocks, and a few specially selected stocks among the other classes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In every instance, when you make a selection, you should consider the) Tj T*
(company's assets, present earnings, and prospective future earnings, and) Tj T*
(then take into consideration all the influences that affect price) Tj T*
(movements, as explained in subsequent chapters.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER VI.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(WHAT STOCKS NOT TO BUY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A great deal more can be said about stocks you should not buy than about) Tj T*
(stocks you should buy, because the list is very much larger.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
