() Tj T*
(Following a period of prosperity comes a period of decline. The first) Tj T*
(sign of it usually is a severe break in the stock market. At that time) Tj T*
(general business is running along at top speed and there is no sign of a) Tj T*
(let-up, but this break in the stock market should be a warning. Most) Tj T*
(people think the break is merely a temporary reaction--they may refer) Tj T*
(to it as a HEALTHY reaction--and they start buying stocks again, and put) Tj T*
(the market up, but it does not go up as high as it was before the break) Tj T*
(occurred. When stock prices do not rally beyond the prices at which they) Tj T*
(were before the break occurred, it is a sign that the turning point has) Tj T*
(been reached and that the bear market has started, although the majority) Tj T*
(of people do not realize this until a long time afterwards.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Next comes a period of depression, when we have low prices, low wages,) Tj T*
(hard times, tight money, and many commercial failures. Many people who) Tj T*
(lost all their money during the speculation period, become thrifty and) Tj T*
(economize during the period of depression, and start in to save again.) Tj T*
(Nearly everybody is pessimistic during this period. Trading on the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange is irregular and as a rule very light.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This is the time to get stock bargains, but the general public as a rule) Tj T*
(doesn't take advantage of it. People are scared and think prices will go) Tj T*
(still lower. The big interests accumulate stocks during this period, and) Tj T*
(sell them during the period of prosperity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER XII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE MONEY MARKET AND STOCK PRICES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Perhaps no other one thing influences the movement of stock prices so) Tj T*
(much, in a large way, as money conditions. It is impossible to have a) Tj T*
(big bull market without plenty of money. During a bull market nearly all) Tj T*
(stocks are bought on margin, which is explained in Chapter XVI. This) Tj T*
(makes it necessary for brokers to borrow large sums of money. When money) Tj T*
(is tight, it is impossible to get enough to carry on a large movement in) Tj T*
(stocks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(You will see, therefore, that the Federal Reserve Bank has it in its) Tj T*
(power to regulate the stock market to some extent. In 1919 speculation) Tj T*
(was carried very much further than it should have been, but undoubtedly) Tj T*
(it would have been much worse had the Federal Reserve Bank not raised) Tj T*
(interest rates and urged member banks to withdraw money from Wall) Tj T*
(Street. While there was considerable criticism of that action, it) Tj T*
(certainly was a good thing for the entire country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In a period of depression, the banks accumulate money, and there always) Tj T*
(is an abundance of money at the beginning of a bull market. During a) Tj T*
(period of prosperity the banks' reserves decrease and their loans) Tj T*
