(CHAPTER XXI.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(STOP LOSS ORDERS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A "stop-loss" is an order to your broker to sell you out if the market) Tj T*
(sells down a certain number of points. Many speculators place stop loss) Tj T*
(orders only two points from the market price. The idea is that when the) Tj T*
(market starts to go down it is likely to continue going down, and by) Tj T*
(taking a two-point loss you may save a much greater loss. It also can be) Tj T*
(applied to a short sale, when you give your broker instructions to buy) Tj T*
(in the stock for you if it goes up a certain number of points.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We read so much in the financial news about stop-loss orders or merely) Tj T*
(stop orders, which is the same thing, the average reader is likely to) Tj T*
(get the idea that it is something he must use for his own protection,) Tj T*
(but it is our opinion that it is something that should be used very) Tj T*
(seldom by those who trade along the broad lines recommended by us. If) Tj T*
(your purchases were made in stocks that were very cheap, you should) Tj T*
(continue to hold them in case of a reaction. If you bought them) Tj T*
(outright or on a substantial margin, you are not in danger, and you) Tj T*
(should look upon your loss merely as a paper loss. In the great majority) Tj T*
(of cases, you will be a great deal better off to hold on to your stocks) Tj T*
(than you would be if you had a stop-loss order.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A large number of stop-loss orders is a good thing for the short) Tj T*
(interests. Let us take U. S. Steel again, as an example. Suppose it is) Tj T*
(selling at 94 and it is believed that there are a large number of) Tj T*
(stop-loss orders at 92. The short interests may sell the stock heavily) Tj T*
ET
