(and force it down to 92. Then the brokers with stop-loss orders would) Tj T*
(begin to sell; that would force the price down still lower, and the) Tj T*
(short interests could buy in to cover at this lower price.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Therefore, we believe that stop-loss orders are a bad thing and, as a) Tj T*
(rule, do not recommend them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is one instance where a stop-loss order can be used to advantage,) Tj T*
(and that is near the top of a bull market. It is impossible to tell when) Tj T*
(the market has reached the top. If you sell out too soon, you may lose a) Tj T*
(profit of several points. Of course, it is better to do that than to) Tj T*
(take a chance of a large loss. In that case, you might instruct your) Tj T*
(broker to place a stop-loss order at two or more points below the) Tj T*
(market, and keep moving it up as the market price moves up. Then when) Tj T*
(the reaction does come, he will sell you out and prevent you from losing) Tj T*
(a large part of your profit. That is about the only instance where we) Tj T*
(recommend a stop-loss order, but we do recommend it to our clients) Tj T*
(sometimes, although seldom.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the stock you own is selling at more than 100 we would suggest that) Tj T*
(you make the stop loss order at least three points from the market, but) Tj T*
(for stocks selling below 100, a two-point stop-loss order might be used.) Tj T*
